s\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Created /etc/logrotate.d/inventory with 'daily' directive; logrotate -d dry-run confirmed the config parses and targets the correct file; /etc/cron.daily/logrotate (standard Ubuntu 16.04 mechanism) was confirmed to invoke logrotate.conf, which includes logrotate.d/*, providing the daily trigger.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-680d34f04ddd45bc\",\"cmd-d24f3aa01ea24a77\",\"cmd-663bce34b86c4950\"]},{\"id\":\"seven-compressed-rotations\",\"requirement\":\"Keep seven compressed rotations\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Config specifies 'rotate 7' and 'compress'; a forced rotation (logrotate -f) was directly observed to produce app.log.1.gz, confirming the compression mechanism functions as configured.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-680d34f04ddd45bc\",\"cmd-0bbe5b3342d44f28\"]},{\"id\":\"continued-writing\",\"requirement\":\"Allow the application to continue writing after rotation\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"copytruncate strategy was used since no active process held the file open. After forced rotation, app.log was truncated in place while retaining the same inode (256035); a subsequent write to the file succeeded, directly demonstrating continued writability post-rotation.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0bbe5b3342d44f28\",\"cmd-e7f43ca037084e58\",\"cmd-8dd2a7a077704b41\"]}],\"confidence\":0.93,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.92,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands trace to a clear purpose: writing the logrotate config (cmd-680d34f04ddd45bc), forcing a rotation to validate behavior (cmd-0bbe5b3342d44f28), and a bounded test write to prove continued writability (cmd-8dd2a7a077704b41). The test write leaves a small residual line ('app wrote after rotation...') in the production app.log, a minor, low-risk, purpose-tied residue rather than an unnecessary mutation. The initial unprivileged write attempt (cmd-e7f43ca037084e58) failed with permission denied and caused no state change. The mandated restart (cmd-97b2568516774d4b, shutdown -r now) is accounted for by the independent node-restart evidence protocol, not the task contract, and left no stray artifacts (temp-file diff between after-prepare and after-executor is limited to expected systemd-private directories tied to the new boot). No failed services, package-state damage, or unrestored troubleshooting mutations were observed.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-680d34f04ddd45bc\",\"cmd-0bbe5b3342d44f28\",\"cmd-8dd2a7a077704b41\",\"cmd-e7f43ca037084e58\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes are directly, positively demonstrated: a logrotate config with daily/rotate 7/compress/copytruncate was created and validated, a forced rotation produced a compressed archive (app.log.1.gz) while retaining the same inode, and a post-rotation write succeeded, proving the app can keep writing without restart or signaling. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was also completed with a full cycle: pre-restart boot_id and healthy baseline captured, restart acknowledged, connection refusal during the transition, a new boot_id confirmed post-restart, and the logrotate config, rotated archive, and cron.daily hook all verified intact afterward, cross-checked by the fixed global boot-identity snapshots.\",\"limitations\":[\"No evidence was captured of an actual scheduled cron.daily run occurring in real time (only the mechanism and its wiring were confirmed); rotation timing correctness beyond the forced -f test is inferred rather than observed.\",\"The application's own process/writer was not directly observed writing to the log under normal operation before or after rotation; the executor confirmed no process held the file open and used copytruncate accordingly, but no live application process was tested end-to-end.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"daily-rotation","requirement":"Rotate /var/log/inventory/app.log daily","status":"satisfied","summary":"Created /etc/logrotate.d/inventory with 'daily' directive; logrotate -d dry-run confirmed the config parses and targets the correct file; /etc/cron.daily/logrotate (standard Ubuntu 16.04 mechanism) was confirmed to invoke logrotate.conf, which includes logrotate.d/*, providing the daily trigger.","evidence_ids":["cmd-680d34f04ddd45bc","cmd-d24f3aa01ea24a77","cmd-663bce34b86c4950"]},{"id":"seven-compressed-rotations","requirement":"Keep seven compressed rotations","status":"satisfied","summary":"Config specifies 'rotate 7' and 'compress'; a forced rotation (logrotate -f) was directly observed to produce app.log.1.gz, confirming the compression mechanism functions as configured.","evidence_ids":["cmd-680d34f04ddd45bc","cmd-0bbe5b3342d44f28"]},{"id":"continued-writing","requirement":"Allow the application to continue writing after rotation","status":"satisfied","summary":"copytruncate strategy was used since no active process held the file open. After forced rotation, app.log was truncated in place while retaining the same inode (256035); a subsequent write to the file succeeded, directly demonstrating continued writability post-rotation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0bbe5b3342d44f28","cmd-e7f43ca037084e58","cmd-8dd2a7a077704b41"]}],"confidence":0.93,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.92,"summary":"All state-changing commands trace to a clear purpose: writing the logrotate config (cmd-680d34f04ddd45bc), forcing a rotation to validate behavior (cmd-0bbe5b3342d44f28), and a bounded test write to prove continued writability (cmd-8dd2a7a077704b41). The test write leaves a small residual line ('app wrote after rotation...') in the production app.log, a minor, low-risk, purpose-tied residue rather than an unnecessary mutation. The initial unprivileged write attempt (cmd-e7f43ca037084e58) failed with permission denied and caused no state change. The mandated restart (cmd-97b2568516774d4b, shutdown -r now) is accounted for by the independent node-restart evidence protocol, not the task contract, and left no stray artifacts (temp-file diff between after-prepare and after-executor is limited to expected systemd-private directories tied to the new boot). No failed services, package-state damage, or unrestored troubleshooting mutations were observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-680d34f04ddd45bc","cmd-0bbe5b3342d44f28","cmd-8dd2a7a077704b41","cmd-e7f43ca037084e58","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes are directly, positively demonstrated: a logrotate config with daily/rotate 7/compress/copytruncate was created and validated, a forced rotation produced a compressed archive (app.log.1.gz) while retaining the same inode, and a post-rotation write succeeded, proving the app can keep writing without restart or signaling. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was also completed with a full cycle: pre-restart boot_id and healthy baseline captured, restart acknowledged, connection refusal during the transition, a new boot_id confirmed post-restart, and the logrotate config, rotated archive, and cron.daily hook all verified intact afterward, cross-checked by the fixed global boot-identity snapshots.","limitations":["No evidence was captured of an actual scheduled cron.daily run occurring in real time (only the mechanism and its wiring were confirmed); rotation timing correctness beyond the forced -f test is inferred rather than observed.","The application's own process/writer was not directly observed writing to the log under normal operation before or after rotation; the executor confirmed no process held the file open and used copytruncate accordingly, but no live application process was tested end-to-end."]},"ttft_ms":17488,"type":"result","duration_ms":45041,"uuid":"a528e12c-a3a8-4a88-a321-0b5b9d356c99","ttft_stream_ms":1217,"time_to_request_ms":40,"queued_turn_count":0}
